      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-181952",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following null sequence:\n\n$a_{n} = ((-1)^{n})/(\\sqrt{n}), n \\ge 1$\n\nLet $\u03b5 > 0$. We wish to find a natural number $N(\u03b5)$ such that for all $n \\ge N$, we have $\\mid a_{n} \\mid < \u03b5$.\n\nComplete the reasoning below, and hence find a suitable $N$ that satisfies this condition.\n\nFirst, notice that for any natural $n \\ge 1$, we have\n\n$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}$ {{field-1}}\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_{n} \\mid < \u03b5$ for $n$:\n\n$1$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$\\sqrt{\u03b5}$ {{field-4}} {{field-5}}"
